1. A Start to Definitions,
1.1. What is a Myth

i. 	Something I, and a few others, have engaged in for a long time in the community 
is working out what a myth truly is. This question is one without an easy answer beyond 
surface-level remarks, and I have thought of it for a lot of my time in the community. To 
go to my simplest and easiest answer, I define a myth as one or more character 
accounts(an account made with a character in mind), on Roblox, who are directly 
associated with one or more games on Roblox. A myth is a character and a game; to me, 
it is good to have such a simple definition. To poke around this definition, many bring up 
the likes of John Doe, Noli, etc, as examples of myths that could not fit the definition, as 
they do not have games directly associated with them. To me, they are not myths; they 
are what some refer to as “legends”, more associated with ARGs or rumors. I see ARGs 
and rumors as too different from what we commonly conceptualize a myth as, but I must 
note that the community’s definition has shifted over time from mysteries, rumors, and 
the like, to a more modern thought of a myth, due to overall quality increases and 
balances for myths. Aside from those, a game without a character account belongs to a 
larger genre, such as a story game or puzzle game. The simple definition works well; I 
haven’t found a myth without a game or without a character account. One could define a 
myth on much stricter ground, but I believe we need the simplest definition, and 
anything which expands on the definition, being a character and a game, is increasing 
the quality of the myth, rather than shifting the definition. We need a wide definition of a 
myth because so many do not believe they are one, or think they need to live up to some 
high standard to even become a myth. Not only this, but people feel disheartened when 
they see a lack of recognition based on surface-level expectations of a myth, hunters 
wanting a specific part of a myth that is not inherent to quality.

