–	For a quality character, there is no doubt that it should be an alternate account, 
not associated with anything outside of the community. To have a character is to create a 
world for yourself, and anything outside of the myth community destroys that feeling. 
Having badges or favorites that are in no way related to myths or hunting takes you out 
of how you should be perceived as a myth, and as a character importantly. The profile is
where it all happens with a myth outside of the game, where people will find your games 
or groups associated, where people may find the characterization in the about section, 
and where people will see your avatar, all key parts of character-building. The quality 
rises for a character the more it is defined in the profile, and how it all can unify into a 
single idea that the myth is conveying to the stalker. This could mean ordering favorites 
or badges, appealing game icons, a solid avatar, and an in-character about section.

iv. 	A game is everything to a myth; it is where every single person who interacts 
with the myth will end up, whether friend or hunter. The quality of a game is something 
hard to have a simpler answer for; in the end, it is all subjective. There are a few major 
areas a myth can cover. To me, what most applies to myth games is either aesthetics, 
gameplay, or lore. So to be an original, quality game, one must focus on their selection 
of those, conveyed uniquely. A myth is very closely related to lore, or a story, as a 
character calls for it. Despite this, many myths choose to avoid any obvious form of 
story, notably MUGEN and other myths that choose to focus on gameplay or aesthetics, 
and are still considered high quality. What is important is to take down the idea that a 
myth must have a story. Aesthetics and gameplay can be quality enough to take the 
lore’s place. The addition of a story is a quick way to a feeling of a built world and 
character, essential for any quality myth, but of course, not the only choice.
  To dig deeper into this idea of aesthetics, gameplay, and lore, one must see one 
concept at a time. Each of those three work to push the creative direction of the myth, 
which will be described in a section on creative expression later on. To begin, a myth’s 
aesthetics is largely regarding a high sense of a world; the building in the game and the 
overall art direction take the hunter into the world, where they will feel what the myth is 
trying to convey. For gameplay, it is simple; how clunky a myth feels to engage with 
matters a lot, and the gameplay includes the ways a myth will convey the world or story, 
whether note, dialogue, NPCs, cutscenes, puzzles, a custom character, mechanics, or 
more. How solidly these work together pushes the art and also pushes the quality of the 
myth upward. Moving on to lore, a myth which focuses on lore has a detailed idea of 
what they will be giving the player; they have dug into the world they have built, and 
developed something for the player to engage with, or follow an arc, and most 
importantly, take away. This means a story provides something to the player, like a new 
thought, context, or perspective that is brought to the player to engage with.

