1.3. What is Art

i.	Something I really want to convey here is how myths connect to art, how we can 
use myths to conceptualize art from our perspective as one of the most unique art 
cultures in the world. Defining art is a much larger topic than the myth community, but 
we can use myths to understand it more. To many, art is a specific set of avenues of 
creative expression, such as painting, writing, sculpting, games, etc. People don’t 
generally need to inquire more about what art is, but to be invested in art is to recognize 
its relevance everywhere. Art is creativity, and because it is, any human activity can be 
artistic, any sport, food, tool, and so forth. Even so, there are limits to art, such as the 
need for function, exemplified by a screwdriver; the functional part does not vary from 
design to design, only the handle varies based on creative choice. From the myth 
community perspective, every myth is art, blatant as that, it is an expression of 
creativity in the form of a myth. Importantly, there is also art in every interaction, every 
hunt, every choice made is a creative choice. This is where function arises again, though; 
groups serve more as function than art, and not all myths can be considered to have the 
most creativity. There are cases where a myth is not a creative pursuit but serves as a 
function for notoriety, some sense of control, or even for monetary gain. Certain parts of 
the community are more for function than art, whether a corruption of motives for 
creation or serving a specific function that allows for little room in artistic choice.

ii. 	 From this idea of art, then comes what art should convey. A good idea of art is 
that it should give a feeling or have some deep meaning. Modern art is criticized due to 
its lack of feeling, and games are also overlooked as what people think of as cheap 
entertainment. Considering creative expression alone as art, we must see that no matter 
what art provides, it is still art. What art can provide varies from form to form, so I’ll 
look into myths as art. A myth, like art, can provide a wide range of things, but the most 
prevalent fall under a few concepts. Art in general can provide (but is not entirely limited 
to) a thought, an experience, or a feeling. Generally, if a myth includes a story, it 
can provide ideas and perspectives to observers, which can also be given through any 
other way of communicating with the player. To give an experience, the functions of the 
game are optimized, and it must additionally have a unique gameplay aspect, like a 
movement system, weapon system, or tool. Additionally, a myth can provide an 
experience of a maze or a town, any world one could build as well. A feeling may arise 
from dialogue, or interaction with characters you can connect to, maybe a story develops 
that arches through to a bitter or sweet end. A myth combines these for their overall art 
direction; some are heavy on concepts, gameplay gimmicks, or connection with the 
player. It all depends on the creative expression they choose to endeavor, and what they 
wish to convey.

